<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Oldenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Oldenburg is a European royal dynasty descended from the medieval counts of Oldenburg in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1448 Count Christian of Oldenburg was elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> as Christian I, and his descendants ruled Denmark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and, for periods, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and Oldenburg itself. Its branches include the Holstein-Gottorp line, which gave rulers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Sweden, and the Glücksburg line, which still reigns in Denmark and Norway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg.jpg" alt="House of Oldenburg" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_County_of_Oldenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian I, elected king of Denmark in 1448 and the first king of the <b>House of Oldenburg</b>, was crowned in Copenhagen Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1448 Count Christian of Oldenburg was elected King of Denmark as Christian I, founding the <b>House of Oldenburg</b>, which later ruled Denmark, Norway, Sweden, Russia and Greece in its various branches.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">County of Oldenburg</a></li></ul></section>
